{
  "dom_id": "step-q351060",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=-\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{4}{0+1}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =4+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{t+1}-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{4}{1+1}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
